
--the reason this test file is in a SQL file and located underneath the "test" model is that, dbt recommends that if you are to create a custom test, 
---it should be listed within the test model, and make sure to also live in a folder called generic. 

----how to write a custom generic test

-- {% test salary_check(model,column_name) %} ---this means the "test" will be to check the salary column of a model. This is the staring of the test block

-- ---custom script in butween the blocks. You want to test for example that none of the salary within this column should be less than 10,000
-- --with this, we need to write a negetive sql query
-- select * from 
-- {{ model }} -- the model name. on the flag, it will take the model name and column name where you will configure this test case.
-- where {{ column_name }} < 10000 --column name and condition. Any column within that column_name will have your data quality checked. If less than 10,000, your test will fail.  

-- {% endtest %} ---end of the test block

---we will then configure this custom test on the generic test yaml file for the employee model. 

Basically, "where {{ column_name }} < 10000" this means if any salary enter into this column is less than 10000, your test will fail. 
That is the negative way of saying all salary within the column should be more than 10000. 
